1
00:00:04,582 --> 00:00:06,174
Your Honor.

2
00:00:09,754 --> 00:00:11,244
Anybody seen the judge?

3
00:00:11,422 --> 00:00:14,186
- Not lately.
- Oh, that could be him.

4
00:00:14,425 --> 00:00:16,893
No. Harry usually twirls more.

5
00:00:22,633 --> 00:00:24,692
- Calendar update, sir.
- Thanks.

6
00:00:24,936 --> 00:00:26,404
My pleasure, sir.

7
00:00:26,572 --> 00:00:28,403
- Charley?
- Sir?

8
00:00:28,774 --> 00:00:31,242
I just danced you down the halls
of the building.

9
00:00:31,410 --> 00:00:33,378
- Yes, sir.
- Doesn't that strike you as odd?

10
00:00:33,545 --> 00:00:34,569
A little.

11
00:00:34,746 --> 00:00:37,909
Most people react with alarm
when I do that kind of stuff.

12
00:00:38,083 --> 00:00:41,814
- I'll try and do better next time, sir.
- Oh, no. That's okay.

13
00:00:41,987 --> 00:00:43,978
I appreciate your indifference.

14
00:00:44,723 --> 00:00:46,554
I hope you like the typing job.

15
00:00:46,725 --> 00:00:48,955
- Looks great.
- I can do it over if you'd like.

16
00:00:49,127 --> 00:00:51,527
Not for my taste.
How's that for a type job, guys?

17
00:00:51,697 --> 00:00:53,062
- Nice!
- Pretty.

18
00:00:53,465 --> 00:00:55,194
Wow. No smudges.

19
00:00:56,068 --> 00:00:58,434
- Keep up the good work.
- I'll try, sir.

20
00:00:59,037 --> 00:01:01,528
- What a nice woman.
- Perfect.

21
00:01:01,740 --> 00:01:05,676
Pretty, obedient, and knows
the meaning of the word "fetch."

22
00:01:06,311 --> 00:01:07,573
Dan!

23
00:01:07,813 --> 00:01:11,011
Come on! She's been running around
like an attention-starved puppy...

24
00:01:11,184 --> 00:01:12,742
...for the last couple of weeks.

25
00:01:12,952 --> 00:01:15,750
Weren't you eager to please
when you were just starting out?

26
00:01:15,922 --> 00:01:18,288
Sucking up to people who can help you
is one thing.

27
00:01:18,458 --> 00:01:21,518
Being nice to everybody is sick.

28
00:01:24,063 --> 00:01:26,156
Hello, gorgeous!

29
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:33,996
- Tell me, what do you need, darling?
- Pack of cigarettes.

30
00:01:34,374 --> 00:01:36,604
They say smoking is bad
for you, you know.

31
00:01:36,776 --> 00:01:41,076
Then give me a plug of tobacco
and a can to spit in.

32
00:01:42,048 --> 00:01:44,642
I know. Who needs a lecture, right?

33
00:01:44,817 --> 00:01:47,718
All I'm saying is that a body like that...

34
00:01:48,121 --> 00:01:50,590
...you should be good to it.

35
00:01:50,891 --> 00:01:53,052
I would be.

36
00:01:54,395 --> 00:01:56,329
I'll find a vending machine.

37
00:01:57,565 --> 00:01:58,793
No. Wait.

38
00:01:59,066 --> 00:02:00,693
Here, Selma.

39
00:02:00,935 --> 00:02:03,062
I know your name.
I know your brand.

40
00:02:03,237 --> 00:02:05,467
- How much?
- They're on the house.

41
00:02:05,639 --> 00:02:07,698
Then I'll take a carton.

42
00:02:09,443 --> 00:02:12,378
Selma, I hope you won't
mind my saying this...

43
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:15,810
...but I've had my eye
on you for quite a while.

44
00:02:16,550 --> 00:02:19,041
I need matches too.

45
00:02:19,553 --> 00:02:22,488
Selma, please. I'm only human.

46
00:02:24,091 --> 00:02:25,456
My name is Bernie.

47
00:02:26,293 --> 00:02:28,386
Bernie the human. Got it.

48
00:02:28,929 --> 00:02:31,523
But wait!
How about some dinner...

49
00:02:31,699 --> 00:02:33,395
...on me?
- Sure.

50
00:02:33,568 --> 00:02:36,969
Next time, my place.

51
00:03:22,485 --> 00:03:25,386
- Well, hello again.
- I got a gun, Bernie.

52
00:03:25,554 --> 00:03:27,954
You look very sexy in that uniform.

53
00:03:28,124 --> 00:03:30,285
Bullets, Bernie. I got those too.

54
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:33,524
I do very well financially, you know.

55
00:03:33,696 --> 00:03:36,358
- Is that so?
- You know that stand in there?

56
00:03:36,632 --> 00:03:38,293
That's only the tip of the iceberg.

57
00:03:38,501 --> 00:03:41,959
I got another one, much bigger.
Over in the municipal building.

58
00:03:42,271 --> 00:03:46,207
I sell more breath mints
than anybody else in the city.

59
00:03:46,475 --> 00:03:48,875
Life in the fast lane, huh?

60
00:03:56,052 --> 00:03:58,180
Ready whenever you're through
rummaging, sir.

61
00:03:58,355 --> 00:04:00,721
- Just a minute.
- Looking for something, Your Honor?

62
00:04:00,891 --> 00:04:02,188
Yeah. My rabbit's foot.

63
00:04:02,359 --> 00:04:04,088
The arrest report on the next case.

64
00:04:04,261 --> 00:04:06,923
- What did it look like?
- It was about this big, fuzzy.

65
00:04:07,097 --> 00:04:08,257
- Green?
- Yeah.

66
00:04:08,432 --> 00:04:10,366
Oh, sir, I threw it out for you.

67
00:04:10,967 --> 00:04:12,559
Threw it out?

68
00:04:12,736 --> 00:04:16,137
Miss Tracy, that was my lucky
rabbit's foot. I had it for over 25 years.

69
00:04:16,306 --> 00:04:18,206
- Oh, my God.
- Now, don't worry, honey.

70
00:04:18,375 --> 00:04:21,606
It was just laying there,
shedding on your papers...

71
00:04:21,778 --> 00:04:23,006
...and smelling funny.

72
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:25,239
Old bunny parts do that.

73
00:04:26,717 --> 00:04:27,877
Sir, don't worry.

74
00:04:28,051 --> 00:04:30,713
I'll find that rabbit's foot
if it's the last thing I do.

75
00:04:30,887 --> 00:04:32,354
Don't worry about it.

76
00:04:32,522 --> 00:04:34,990
It was just a silly old thing, anyway.
I'll be fine.

77
00:04:44,102 --> 00:04:47,128
Cancel my hang-gliding lesson,
okay, Charley?

78
00:04:48,339 --> 00:04:52,002
If this is gonna take a while,
I need to hit the head before we start.

79
00:04:52,410 --> 00:04:53,741
Who might that be?

80
00:04:53,911 --> 00:04:56,641
Mitchell Bowers, Your Honor.
The defendant, my client.

81
00:04:56,814 --> 00:04:58,304
The arrest report, sir.

82
00:04:59,617 --> 00:05:01,551
Breaking and entering, Mr. Bowers.

83
00:05:01,719 --> 00:05:05,348
I'm innocent. How can you arrest a man
for breaking and entering his own home?

84
00:05:05,723 --> 00:05:06,951
Mr. Prosecutor?

85
00:05:07,125 --> 00:05:10,492
Your Honor, we have
a bit of a legal gray area here.

86
00:05:10,995 --> 00:05:14,829
- How gray?
- You see, Mr. Bowers here is dead.

87
00:05:16,601 --> 00:05:19,035
In the old days, we used to bury them.

88
00:05:19,203 --> 00:05:22,605
Then they never gave any trouble again.

89
00:05:23,909 --> 00:05:28,369
Well, Your Honor, Mr. Bowers bought
the farm in the legal sense a year ago, June.

90
00:05:28,547 --> 00:05:30,811
When his wife had him declared dead.

91
00:05:30,983 --> 00:05:34,419
Mitch? When I got that letter about you,
I had to assume...

92
00:05:34,586 --> 00:05:36,986
...the government knew what
they were talking about.

93
00:05:37,156 --> 00:05:38,384
That's your first mistake.

94
00:05:38,590 --> 00:05:39,818
Have a seat, pumpkin.

95
00:05:39,992 --> 00:05:42,358
Marrying that little twerp
was your second.

96
00:05:42,761 --> 00:05:44,729
- Miss Williams.
- Your Honor...

97
00:05:44,930 --> 00:05:47,797
...my client served with the Marine Corps
in Vietnam.

98
00:05:47,966 --> 00:05:51,231
He was presumed to have been
killed in action in 1973...

99
00:05:51,403 --> 00:05:54,429
...but in reality, he was captured
by an enemy patrol...

100
00:05:54,606 --> 00:05:57,507
...and spent the next several years
in one of their prisons.

101
00:05:57,676 --> 00:05:59,769
While I'm busting my butt trying
to escape...

102
00:05:59,945 --> 00:06:03,745
...she hires some lawyer to snuff me out
so she can marry that dipstick.

103
00:06:04,417 --> 00:06:05,975
The dipstick in question...

104
00:06:06,152 --> 00:06:09,918
Mr. Duane Sedgewick,
the lady's present husband.

105
00:06:10,156 --> 00:06:11,680
Would you all approach the bench?

106
00:06:11,858 --> 00:06:16,557
Why are we wasting time?
Just let me and him arm-wrestle for her.

107
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:20,695
Your Honor, I can understand
Mr. Bowers' pique.

108
00:06:20,867 --> 00:06:23,097
This has been quite a shock
for me as well as...

109
00:06:23,269 --> 00:06:24,736
Stow it, four-eyes.

110
00:06:26,206 --> 00:06:28,766
Four-eyes? How archaic.

111
00:06:28,942 --> 00:06:30,603
That'll be enough name-calling.

112
00:06:30,777 --> 00:06:32,802
Go on, Mr. Dipstick...
I mean...

113
00:06:34,781 --> 00:06:37,147
Your Honor,
I'd like to clear up this notion...

114
00:06:37,317 --> 00:06:39,080
...that I'm some sort of home wrecker.

115
00:06:39,252 --> 00:06:41,482
That I seduced Nora while he was away.

116
00:06:41,654 --> 00:06:43,485
That couldn't be farther
from the truth.

117
00:06:43,756 --> 00:06:45,621
Like we used to say
in the Corps:

118
00:06:45,792 --> 00:06:47,556
"Any port in a storm." Right, Nora?

119
00:06:47,728 --> 00:06:50,196
I am telling you, it was not physical.

120
00:06:50,364 --> 00:06:52,832
Not for a long time.

121
00:06:53,834 --> 00:06:58,100
Mitch, from the time you left the States,
I was completely...

122
00:06:58,272 --> 00:07:00,866
...you know, celibate...

123
00:07:01,041 --> 00:07:02,770
...for over nine years.

124
00:07:02,977 --> 00:07:04,638
Say what?

125
00:07:05,813 --> 00:07:08,748
And all those years, I loved you.

126
00:07:08,916 --> 00:07:10,383
- I still do.
- Great.

127
00:07:10,551 --> 00:07:12,781
Then dump Mr. Potato Head here
and let's go home.

128
00:07:13,721 --> 00:07:16,087
- I can't do that.
- Why not, Nora?

129
00:07:16,257 --> 00:07:17,519
Because.

130
00:07:18,125 --> 00:07:19,615
I love him too.

131
00:07:20,961 --> 00:07:23,225
I understand that
there's a lot of heartache...

132
00:07:23,397 --> 00:07:25,297
...and personal anguish involved here.

133
00:07:25,466 --> 00:07:28,903
But I'm afraid all I can deal with is
the charge before me. Miss Tracy?

134
00:07:29,838 --> 00:07:33,069
- Miss Tracy?
- Still looking for the foot, sir.

135
00:07:33,241 --> 00:07:36,938
Would you mind putting the garbage down
and finding me the summary of damages?

136
00:07:37,112 --> 00:07:38,101
Right away.

137
00:07:38,280 --> 00:07:40,305
Would the counsels approach the bench?

138
00:07:41,149 --> 00:07:43,617
I'm afraid there's not much
we can do here, guys.

139
00:07:43,785 --> 00:07:45,753
- This is a case for a civil court.
- Agreed.

140
00:07:45,921 --> 00:07:48,287
But if the prosecution
would drop the charges...

141
00:07:48,456 --> 00:07:50,947
- Summary of the damages, sir.
- These are sticky.

142
00:07:51,126 --> 00:07:53,686
- Could be jelly.
- That'll come right out.

143
00:07:53,862 --> 00:07:56,023
- No, that just smears it.
- You missed a spot.

144
00:07:56,231 --> 00:07:58,199
It's fine. It's fine.
It's great.

145
00:07:58,366 --> 00:08:00,527
The saliva has removed
most of the jelly.

146
00:08:00,702 --> 00:08:04,001
What are the locks for? You never
needed them when I was around.

147
00:08:04,172 --> 00:08:06,936
- You never had antiques.
- Never had any use for them.

148
00:08:07,108 --> 00:08:08,803
Never had use for poachers, either.

149
00:08:08,977 --> 00:08:11,742
- I got a mind...
- lf you have, I've seen no evidence of it.

150
00:08:11,914 --> 00:08:13,211
Oh, honeys, please!

151
00:08:13,382 --> 00:08:16,874
You got 24 hours to get you and
your antiques out of my apartment.

152
00:08:17,053 --> 00:08:18,748
Will both of you
please stop it?!

153
00:08:18,921 --> 00:08:20,445
Mrs. Sedgewick.

154
00:08:20,623 --> 00:08:23,990
I'm... I'm sorry, Your Honor. I really am.

155
00:08:24,460 --> 00:08:26,758
I knew she was gonna do that.
I felt it coming on.

156
00:08:26,929 --> 00:08:29,022
I mean, I felt... I knew it.

157
00:08:29,765 --> 00:08:32,598
You see, I don't wanna hurt
either one of them.

158
00:08:32,802 --> 00:08:34,565
They're good men.

159
00:08:35,104 --> 00:08:37,265
They both, in their own ways...

160
00:08:37,506 --> 00:08:40,373
...have made me
the happiest woman in the world.

161
00:08:40,977 --> 00:08:43,605
- Come on, now, honey. Stop it.
- Don't cry, Nora.

162
00:08:43,779 --> 00:08:46,009
Take your hands off her,
or we'll end this quick.

163
00:08:46,182 --> 00:08:51,085
Oh, yes, violence! Just the sort of behavior
I'd expect from a borderline Homo sapien.

164
00:08:51,287 --> 00:08:54,815
Why, you...
I never even hugged my father!

165
00:08:54,992 --> 00:08:57,460
All right. I've had enough!

166
00:08:57,627 --> 00:09:01,028
So have I. Can't you see
that Nora is mine now?

167
00:09:01,264 --> 00:09:03,755
- Over my dead body!
- Lf you two gentlemen...

168
00:09:04,768 --> 00:09:06,827
- He's out cold!
- Get the ice.

169
00:09:07,004 --> 00:09:09,097
There's some in my refrigerator. I'll get it!

170
00:09:16,847 --> 00:09:20,112
Brown bag, tuna. Help yourself.

171
00:09:22,285 --> 00:09:23,912
Did I mention it was
on whole wheat?

172
00:09:29,793 --> 00:09:32,353
All right, can we sit up, Mr. Fielding?

173
00:09:32,729 --> 00:09:35,027
- There you go.
- There we are.

174
00:09:36,901 --> 00:09:39,369
- I can't breathe.
- That's normal.

175
00:09:39,804 --> 00:09:42,398
Not in my family.

176
00:09:43,074 --> 00:09:46,202
- How is he, doc?
- He took a real nasty bump on the snoot.

177
00:09:47,011 --> 00:09:50,538
That's based on what, nine,
10 years of higher education?

178
00:09:51,582 --> 00:09:53,140
It's definitely broken.

179
00:09:53,317 --> 00:09:56,013
- Thanks for taking care of it.
- It should be all right...

180
00:09:56,187 --> 00:09:58,587
...but we ought to get x-rays
as soon as possible.

181
00:09:58,756 --> 00:10:03,159
I mean, if it didn't set right,
we would have to re-break it, wouldn't we?

182
00:10:06,264 --> 00:10:08,732
- Dan, are you okay to work?
- Yeah, I suppose so.

183
00:10:08,900 --> 00:10:13,837
Just as long as I stay away from words
like "small claims" or "Smithsonian."

184
00:10:15,106 --> 00:10:16,437
- I'm sorry.
- You know...

185
00:10:16,607 --> 00:10:19,839
...I have a little spinal irregularity
you might find amusing also.

186
00:10:21,647 --> 00:10:23,342
Charley, can we try to rearrange...?

187
00:10:23,816 --> 00:10:25,113
Charley?

188
00:10:25,284 --> 00:10:28,082
No trace.
Not a piece of fur, nothing.

189
00:10:28,487 --> 00:10:31,513
Forget about the rabbit's foot.
It was an accident. It's gone.

190
00:10:31,690 --> 00:10:33,214
Don't worry about it, I'm not.

191
00:10:33,392 --> 00:10:34,791
What I did was unforgivable.

192
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:37,758
It's forgivable.
Look, I'm not upset. I'm smiling.

193
00:10:38,363 --> 00:10:40,297
I wouldn't blame you
if you fired me.

194
00:10:40,532 --> 00:10:45,196
Charley, I'm happy with you,
and I'm happy with your work. Okay?

195
00:10:45,971 --> 00:10:48,633
- Thank you, sir.
- Yuck!

196
00:10:50,209 --> 00:10:53,372
Tuna juice. Leaky bag in the garbage.

197
00:10:53,612 --> 00:10:55,842
No, it's okay. I'm left-handed.

198
00:10:56,014 --> 00:10:57,641
- Your Honor?
- Yeah, Bull?

199
00:10:58,250 --> 00:11:00,377
Your Honor, I put...

200
00:11:02,589 --> 00:11:04,853
- I put Bowers in the holding cell.
- Good.

201
00:11:05,024 --> 00:11:08,482
I wanna see him and the Sedgewicks
in my chambers right after I wash up.

202
00:11:08,661 --> 00:11:10,094
I was hoping you'd say that.

203
00:11:11,764 --> 00:11:13,254
Fish.

204
00:11:14,167 --> 00:11:15,725
Duck.

205
00:11:19,806 --> 00:11:22,070
- Bernie?
- I knew you'd come back.

206
00:11:22,242 --> 00:11:23,470
You got any aspirin?

207
00:11:23,643 --> 00:11:25,076
Extra-strength or regular?

208
00:11:25,245 --> 00:11:27,770
If you don't see what you want,
I can order it for you.

209
00:11:27,981 --> 00:11:30,541
Bernie, It's not for me. It's for the DA.

210
00:11:30,717 --> 00:11:33,584
And please, stop making a fuss over me.

211
00:11:33,753 --> 00:11:35,744
I am not interested.

212
00:11:36,256 --> 00:11:38,884
Selma, I feel I have a lot to offer you.

213
00:11:39,425 --> 00:11:41,791
For example, companionship.

214
00:11:41,961 --> 00:11:44,522
That's why God gave us TV.

215
00:11:46,233 --> 00:11:48,701
Selma, you're the kind of person...

216
00:11:48,869 --> 00:11:51,099
...I could live out the golden years with.

217
00:11:51,705 --> 00:11:55,300
Bernie, I'm sure you're
a nice guy and all that...

218
00:11:55,476 --> 00:11:57,967
...but I am not looking for a man.

219
00:11:58,412 --> 00:12:01,939
Did I mention the two Plymouths
and the life insurance policy?

220
00:12:02,550 --> 00:12:06,418
Bernie, I want to be alone.

221
00:12:07,922 --> 00:12:10,015
Selma, are you trying
to tell me something?

222
00:12:15,896 --> 00:12:17,124
You again, huh?

223
00:12:18,666 --> 00:12:21,499
You know, this intimidation
stuff is just not working.

224
00:12:21,802 --> 00:12:23,292
So I lost my rabbit's foot.

225
00:12:23,470 --> 00:12:26,565
So what? It was more of a keepsake
than a lucky charm, anyway.

226
00:12:26,741 --> 00:12:28,675
I'm just not that superstitious.

227
00:12:28,843 --> 00:12:31,141
Besides, no bad luck has befallen me...

228
00:12:31,313 --> 00:12:36,046
...unless you want to count some jelly
on some papers or some tuna...

229
00:12:37,586 --> 00:12:39,053
Roll over.

230
00:12:39,221 --> 00:12:40,916
Roll over!

231
00:12:41,289 --> 00:12:43,587
Give me a dog any day.

232
00:13:05,180 --> 00:13:09,618
The judge would like to see you two
in his chambers, right through that door.

233
00:13:16,626 --> 00:13:18,093
Yeah?

234
00:13:19,395 --> 00:13:21,158
- Here he is, Your Honor.
- Thanks, Bull.

235
00:13:21,330 --> 00:13:22,763
- You need me?
- I don't think so.

236
00:13:22,932 --> 00:13:25,127
We're not gonna have
any more punching, are we?

237
00:13:25,301 --> 00:13:26,768
- No, sir.
- It's okay.

238
00:13:30,139 --> 00:13:32,937
Hey, man, I'm sorry.
It was just one of those things.

239
00:13:33,109 --> 00:13:36,272
You broke one of those things.

240
00:13:38,314 --> 00:13:41,750
- You wanted to see us, Your Honor?
- Yes. I wanted to see you all.

241
00:13:42,685 --> 00:13:44,482
Baby, are you all right?

242
00:13:44,654 --> 00:13:46,485
Yeah, I'm fine. I'm fine.

243
00:13:48,257 --> 00:13:49,919
Here's the aspirin.

244
00:13:51,094 --> 00:13:52,356
Is that comfortable?

245
00:13:52,529 --> 00:13:54,190
It's supposed to help drainage.

246
00:13:55,265 --> 00:13:57,631
I have a lot of internal weeping.

247
00:13:58,402 --> 00:14:01,735
Come on, Dan. Let's go find
a place for you to lie down.

248
00:14:01,905 --> 00:14:03,770
Okay. Don't let go.

249
00:14:03,940 --> 00:14:07,103
I won't. I won't. Careful.
There's a little warp in the floor.

250
00:14:10,814 --> 00:14:13,544
So we've got a real toughie here,
don't we?

251
00:14:13,750 --> 00:14:17,550
- Not really, judge, the way I see it.
- That was a rhetorical question, Mr. Bowers.

252
00:14:17,721 --> 00:14:19,188
I'd like a chance to answer it.

253
00:14:20,524 --> 00:14:22,014
A few good men.

254
00:14:22,192 --> 00:14:24,319
Hey, look, buddy.
You stole my wife, okay?

255
00:14:24,494 --> 00:14:26,189
But don't be knocking the Corps!

256
00:14:26,363 --> 00:14:27,591
Put a lid on it.

257
00:14:27,931 --> 00:14:30,627
Now, Mrs. Sedgewick...

258
00:14:32,036 --> 00:14:33,333
...it's up to you.

259
00:14:34,072 --> 00:14:37,166
- What is?
- You've gotta choose between these men.

260
00:14:37,642 --> 00:14:39,166
Will it be Mitch...

261
00:14:41,145 --> 00:14:43,636
...or husband number two?

262
00:14:49,354 --> 00:14:50,685
I don't...

263
00:14:51,255 --> 00:14:53,120
I don't know!

264
00:14:53,391 --> 00:14:56,622
They are two completely different men...

265
00:14:56,794 --> 00:14:59,058
...and yet I love them both.

266
00:14:59,430 --> 00:15:00,897
How do you measure love?

267
00:15:01,065 --> 00:15:02,828
- Well, in boot camp, we...
- Mitch.

268
00:15:03,601 --> 00:15:04,932
Rhetorical again, huh?

269
00:15:06,003 --> 00:15:08,130
But I think I've got an answer
to this problem.

270
00:15:08,373 --> 00:15:10,170
- You do?
- Simple, really.

271
00:15:10,341 --> 00:15:13,174
You're gonna feel silly
you didn't think of it yourselves.

272
00:15:13,344 --> 00:15:16,178
You two guys share her.

273
00:15:16,482 --> 00:15:18,746
- What?!
- What do you think?

274
00:15:18,984 --> 00:15:20,315
Mitch?

275
00:15:20,486 --> 00:15:22,920
This is the kind you answer.

276
00:15:23,222 --> 00:15:24,553
Off the record?

277
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:27,217
I think your porch light's
burned out, judge.

278
00:15:27,426 --> 00:15:30,418
- Duane?
- I gotta go along with Mitch on that one.

279
00:15:30,596 --> 00:15:33,064
This may seem
a little untraditional at first...

280
00:15:33,232 --> 00:15:34,995
...but trust me,
you'll get used to it.

281
00:15:35,167 --> 00:15:37,658
It may mean an extra chair
at the breakfast table...

282
00:15:37,836 --> 00:15:40,236
...or working out
a sleeping schedule, but, hey...

283
00:15:40,506 --> 00:15:43,907
...we're talking about
Nora's happiness, aren't we?

284
00:15:46,078 --> 00:15:48,171
I love two people.

285
00:15:49,014 --> 00:15:52,279
That doesn't sound
as bad as it feels.

286
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:56,382
Yeah.

287
00:15:56,956 --> 00:16:00,551
- Your Honor, l...
- Charley, I want you to draft a document.

288
00:16:00,727 --> 00:16:02,194
This is nothing official, folks.

289
00:16:02,362 --> 00:16:04,421
We'll just rough out a sample agreement.

290
00:16:04,597 --> 00:16:07,566
You can take it to your lawyers,
have it modified, add clauses.

291
00:16:07,734 --> 00:16:09,201
I'll get a pad.

292
00:16:09,469 --> 00:16:10,868
Miss Tracy?

293
00:16:11,171 --> 00:16:12,468
Your dress.

294
00:16:13,239 --> 00:16:14,467
Beef stew.

295
00:16:15,442 --> 00:16:17,910
You've been in the swill again,
haven't you?

296
00:16:19,446 --> 00:16:22,210
Everybody ready?
We'll start off with place of residence...

297
00:16:22,382 --> 00:16:26,113
...and move on to nocturnal visitation...
- That won't be necessary, Your Honor.

298
00:16:26,286 --> 00:16:28,254
- It won't?
- No.

299
00:16:28,421 --> 00:16:30,286
I've decided to step out of the picture.

300
00:16:32,892 --> 00:16:35,861
- Is this going to be a contract or...
- I'm working.

301
00:16:36,029 --> 00:16:38,156
- Then I should just...
- Watch.

302
00:16:38,832 --> 00:16:40,198
What are you saying?

303
00:16:41,435 --> 00:16:44,529
I'm saying I just can't stand
to see you suffer anymore, Nora.

304
00:16:44,805 --> 00:16:46,796
I'll never love anyone
the way I love you...

305
00:16:46,974 --> 00:16:50,000
...but I'd rather give you up
than make your life miserable.

306
00:16:51,178 --> 00:16:52,611
Mitch...

307
00:16:53,280 --> 00:16:54,577
...she's all yours.

308
00:16:55,115 --> 00:16:57,481
- That's real decent of you, Dobie.
- Duane.

309
00:16:57,751 --> 00:16:58,979
Oh, whatever.

310
00:17:03,090 --> 00:17:06,082
You're just giving me up,
just like that?

311
00:17:06,460 --> 00:17:09,020
Yep. Just like that.

312
00:17:09,563 --> 00:17:11,190
How can you do that?

313
00:17:11,365 --> 00:17:13,526
Don't you want to put up
a fight or anything?

314
00:17:13,701 --> 00:17:16,363
Duane is putting up a fight, Nora,
with himself.

315
00:17:16,637 --> 00:17:18,867
Takes a big man to do what he just did.

316
00:17:19,139 --> 00:17:20,367
Doesn't it, Mitch?

317
00:17:21,175 --> 00:17:22,404
Yeah.

318
00:17:24,246 --> 00:17:26,077
Yeah, it does.

319
00:17:27,215 --> 00:17:28,546
You love her that much, huh?

320
00:17:29,417 --> 00:17:30,645
I do.

321
00:17:31,820 --> 00:17:33,082
Yeah...

322
00:17:34,656 --> 00:17:36,556
...I guess you do.

323
00:17:38,627 --> 00:17:41,494
What does this guy do?
Does he stay home a lot or what?

324
00:17:41,663 --> 00:17:44,894
He's pretty much a homebody,
I guess you'd say.

325
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:48,699
He likes to work around the apartment
in his spare time.

326
00:17:49,004 --> 00:17:50,471
Not like me, huh?

327
00:17:51,907 --> 00:17:53,807
No, not like you.

328
00:17:54,109 --> 00:17:56,475
Yeah, I could never get into
all that stuff...

329
00:17:56,845 --> 00:17:59,177
Fixing windows and puttering
around the house.

330
00:17:59,781 --> 00:18:02,147
Well, it's tough to putter
when you're gone...

331
00:18:02,317 --> 00:18:04,445
...for two and three years
at a stretch.

332
00:18:05,555 --> 00:18:07,022
Never got used to it, huh?

333
00:18:09,625 --> 00:18:10,922
Never.

334
00:18:15,798 --> 00:18:17,095
We'll stay in touch.

335
00:18:18,367 --> 00:18:19,629
Yeah.

336
00:18:19,802 --> 00:18:21,531
We'll stay in touch.

337
00:18:22,705 --> 00:18:25,265
Sir, how did you get them talking?

338
00:18:25,474 --> 00:18:28,773
Easy. I just made an absurd suggestion.

339
00:18:29,579 --> 00:18:30,841
Didn't Solomon do that?

340
00:18:31,147 --> 00:18:33,877
Real short judge,
works the day shift, right?

341
00:18:39,522 --> 00:18:41,183
Good luck to you, old man.

342
00:18:43,159 --> 00:18:44,626
You mean?

343
00:18:45,161 --> 00:18:46,652
You're a lucky guy.

344
00:18:47,631 --> 00:18:49,929
I know. Thanks, Mitch.

345
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:52,461
But if I ever hear you've been
mistreating her...

346
00:18:52,636 --> 00:18:56,231
Don't be silly. I'm not as stupid as I look.

347
00:18:56,406 --> 00:18:57,668
You're crushing my hand.

348
00:19:01,078 --> 00:19:02,636
Well, judge...

349
00:19:03,480 --> 00:19:04,777
...about those charges...

350
00:19:04,948 --> 00:19:07,382
There are no charges
if nobody's pressing them.

351
00:19:07,551 --> 00:19:09,576
I don't see anybody pressing them.

352
00:19:09,753 --> 00:19:12,415
- Then I can go now?
- Sure. I'll clear it with the DA.

353
00:19:12,589 --> 00:19:14,147
Hey, thanks, judge.

354
00:19:15,525 --> 00:19:17,254
Hey, listen.

355
00:19:17,661 --> 00:19:19,686
I don't wanna sound like a heel...

356
00:19:19,863 --> 00:19:22,331
...but I got this wandering blood
in me, you know...

357
00:19:22,766 --> 00:19:25,030
...and I know I can't
stay home forever.

358
00:19:26,436 --> 00:19:28,371
And most women need the other kind.

359
00:19:29,073 --> 00:19:31,701
You're a pretty big guy
yourself, Mr. Bowers.

360
00:19:31,943 --> 00:19:33,376
Yeah. And I'm also dead, judge.

361
00:19:33,611 --> 00:19:35,704
Right. No identification.

362
00:19:35,947 --> 00:19:38,814
- No records.
- No taxes, no bills.

363
00:19:39,183 --> 00:19:42,050
You should let somebody know.
You've gotta earn a living.

364
00:19:42,220 --> 00:19:45,451
<i>Well, I'm checking the classified
of Soldier of Fortune magazine.</i>

365
00:19:45,690 --> 00:19:47,555
Gotta be a war going on somewhere.

366
00:19:48,860 --> 00:19:50,487
Adios, judge.

367
00:19:52,096 --> 00:19:53,324
Mitch?

368
00:19:56,768 --> 00:19:58,258
Take care.

369
00:19:58,569 --> 00:20:00,036
You too.

370
00:20:00,972 --> 00:20:02,200
I like him.

371
00:20:03,374 --> 00:20:06,537
But if I were you,
I'd tattoo a little personality on him.

372
00:20:15,954 --> 00:20:17,319
Get the idea?

373
00:20:25,798 --> 00:20:27,197
Ready, pumpkin?

374
00:20:27,533 --> 00:20:28,864
All set, Dobie.

375
00:20:29,168 --> 00:20:30,965
- Duane.
- Oh, Duane.

376
00:20:34,339 --> 00:20:35,636
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

377
00:20:35,841 --> 00:20:37,706
Yes, judge, thank you.

378
00:20:38,077 --> 00:20:39,442
You've been awfully...

379
00:20:40,045 --> 00:20:41,376
Strange.

380
00:20:41,547 --> 00:20:42,878
As long as you know.

381
00:20:44,383 --> 00:20:46,874
- Goodbye.
- Goodbye, now.

382
00:20:50,622 --> 00:20:54,616
Oh, sir, you handled that
with great skill.

383
00:20:54,794 --> 00:20:56,318
I just got lucky.

384
00:20:58,164 --> 00:21:00,189
I knew you would.

385
00:21:09,075 --> 00:21:10,474
Oh, hello.

386
00:21:11,077 --> 00:21:13,204
What did I tell you
about this stuff?

387
00:21:13,446 --> 00:21:15,846
I'm sorry. I sent it earlier.

388
00:21:16,149 --> 00:21:17,446
I couldn't stop it.

389
00:21:18,384 --> 00:21:20,443
Don't worry.
I won't bother you anymore.

390
00:21:20,754 --> 00:21:22,415
Don't get depressed on me.

391
00:21:22,622 --> 00:21:24,249
No. I'll be all right. Really.

392
00:21:24,424 --> 00:21:26,153
Come on, Bernie. Chins up.

393
00:21:28,528 --> 00:21:29,790
You know...

394
00:21:30,029 --> 00:21:33,931
...nobody's sent me flowers
in 25 years.

395
00:21:34,467 --> 00:21:36,663
- I can't believe that.
- It's true.

396
00:21:37,104 --> 00:21:39,538
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

397
00:21:39,774 --> 00:21:42,265
But what you said about relationships...

398
00:21:42,443 --> 00:21:44,206
...living out the golden years...

399
00:21:44,512 --> 00:21:47,072
- Yeah?
- I'm still not interested.

400
00:21:48,315 --> 00:21:49,577
But dinner?

401
00:21:50,050 --> 00:21:52,382
- You'll go with me?
- I'll go with you...

402
00:21:52,553 --> 00:21:55,647
...but no more marriage talk.
- You got it.

403
00:21:56,257 --> 00:22:00,125
But I can't promise that
I won't get affectionate.

404
00:22:00,528 --> 00:22:04,089
Bring a box of Reggie Bars,
and you got a shot.

405
00:22:23,518 --> 00:22:24,951
Here you are, fella.

406
00:22:26,488 --> 00:22:29,616
Well, 18 floors
into a nice, soft mailbag.

407
00:22:29,958 --> 00:22:32,688
You only got eight left, pal.

408
00:22:34,229 --> 00:22:35,958
- Good evening, Your Honor.
- Hi, Liz.

409
00:22:36,331 --> 00:22:38,731
- Dan, how's the nose?
- It's all right.

410
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:42,834
I figured if I stay off of it a week or so,
it'll heal very nicely. Thank you.

411
00:22:43,405 --> 00:22:44,736
Hi, everybody.

412
00:22:44,906 --> 00:22:46,533
Hi, Selma. How did it go?

413
00:22:46,708 --> 00:22:47,970
How did what go?

414
00:22:48,276 --> 00:22:50,744
Oh, come on, Selma.
Don't keep us all in suspense.

415
00:22:50,912 --> 00:22:52,470
How did the date go with Bernie?

416
00:22:52,647 --> 00:22:53,875
Oh, that.

417
00:22:54,049 --> 00:22:57,280
You don't have to give us all the details.
What time did you get home?

418
00:22:57,619 --> 00:23:00,453
Well, if you must know, very early.

419
00:23:00,623 --> 00:23:02,352
You didn't have a good time?

420
00:23:02,525 --> 00:23:03,787
I didn't say that.

421
00:23:03,960 --> 00:23:08,454
- You said you got home early.
- I got home around midnight.

422
00:23:08,965 --> 00:23:10,796
Bernie got home around noon.

423
00:23:13,703 --> 00:23:15,466
Ready whenever you are.

